19-                                                                                          1. Introduction
20-
21-                                              Let X be a smooth complex variety and D be an effective Q-divisor on X. The multiplier ideal
22-                                           associated to D is defined as
23-                                           (1)                                     I(D) = ϕ∗ OY (KY /X − [ϕ∗ D])
24-                                           where ϕ : Y → X is a log resolution of (X, D) (see [Laz04, Definition 9.2.1]). Multiplier ideals
25-                                           and their vanishing theorems are very useful in many areas, but an explicit formula for multiplier
26-                                           ideals is hard to give except in several special cases, see for example [Bli04], [How11], [Mus06],
27-                                           [Tei07], [Tei08], [Tho14], [Tho16]. On the other hand, singularities of plane curves have been
28-                                           studied for a long time and there is a main way to describe them by using infinitely near points,
29:                                           Puiseux series, and invariants such as characteristic exponents, multiplicity sequences, intersection
30-                                           multiplicities, etc. In this case, there are many results that describe partial information associated to
31-                                           multiplier ideals, like the log-canonical threshold or more generally jumping numbers, by analyzing
32-                                           characteristic exponents or the contribution of exceptional divisors of a resolution of singularity, see
33-                                           [AMD16], [GHM16], [HJ18], [Igu77], [Jär07], [Jär11], [Kuw99], [Nai09], [ST06], [Tuc10b], [Sai00].
34-                                           By analyzing properties and invariants of infinitely near points, such as proximity, free (or satellite)
35:                                           points and multiplicity sequences, and using unique factorization theorems for complete primary
36-                                           valuation ideals in a regular local ring of dimension 2, Järvilehto [Jär11] provided a thorough
37-                                           description of the jumping numbers of multiplier ideals of a simple complete ideal, in particular,
38-                                           of an analytically irreducible plane curve. In [HJ18], the authors generalize the formula to any
39-                                           complete ideal in a regular local ring of dimension 2. Taking a different path, Naie [Nai09] developed
40-                                           independently a complete description of the jumping numbers for analytically irreducible plane
41-                                           curves in terms of the Zariski exponents. From a different perspective, Tucker [Tuc10b] presented
42-                                           an algorithm to find jumping numbers for any plane curve. In [AMD16], the authors improved
43-                                           Tucker’s algorithm to compute more efficiently the jumping numbers of any ideal a in a two-
44-                                           dimensional local ring OX,O with a rational singularity. Moreover, given a fixed log resolution
45-                                           ϕ : X ′ → X of a and any jumping number λ of a, this algorithm managed to compute an antinef
46-                                           divisor Dλ,ϕ such that
47-                                                                                     I(aλ ) = ϕ∗ OX ′ (−Dλ,ϕ )
