69-   By describing multiplier ideals completely, we also derive a formula for all the jumping numbers
70-of Z in Corollary 3.2, which recovers [Jär11, Theorem 9.4]. A complete calculation using the method
71-in this paper is given in Example 3.4.
72-   We have recently learned of the papers [AMB17] and [Dur18]. In [AMB17], the authors provided
73-a method to explicitly compute any complete ideal in a smooth complex surface. In particular,
74-their algorithm gives an explicit description of multiplier ideals of irreducible plane curves in terms
75-of maximal contact elements (see [AMB17, §2.4]). In the thesis [Dur18], the author developed
76-another method to get the same formula by an algorithm inspired by [Tuc10a] and [How11]. Our
77-construction of standard factors coincides with a special choice of maximal contact elements of Z
78-defined in [AMB17, §2.4] and approximate roots defined in [Dur18, Chapter 2 §1] [VD19, Definition
79-3.2]. These irreducible polynomials have been defined in the literature classically, see for example
80-[AM73a] [AM73b]. The formula for the generators of multiplier ideals of irreducible plane curves is
81-the same as in the papers above, but we use a different method to show these elements are enough
82-to generate the ideal, which is hopefully of independent interest. While the algorithms in [AMD16]
83-and[AMB17] are based on Lipman’s unique factorization theorem (see [Lip94, Corollary 3.1]), our
84-method uses a computational result of Järvilehto (see [Jär11, Proposition 7.14]) and an algorithm
85-to construct of a standard form of the equation of the germ.
