        where d0 = n, di = gcd(mi , . . . , m1 , n), mi ∈ / (di−1 ) and dg−1 > dg = 1. Then the sequence
        (n; m1 , . . . , mg ) is called the characteristic sequence of S. Given any germ of irreducible
        plane curve singularity Z, we define the characteristic sequence of Z to be the characteristic
        sequence of any y-root of Z. It is well defined since all y-roots of the irreducible curve
        Z are conjugate by Lemma 2.2 and hence have the same characteristic sequence. The
        characteristic sequence of Z is independent of the choice of coordinates x, y around O as
        long as Z is not tangent to the y-axis.
     • For the germ of a plane curve Z, there exists a unique “smallest” resolution of the singularity
       which is a birational morphism π consisted by the smallest number of blow-ups
