2.4. Proximity matrix and multiplier ideals.
    We recall the proximity relation of inifinitely near points and its connection to multiplier ideals.

Definition 2.9. [Cas00, §3.3] Let O be a point on a smooth surface Y . We call points on the
exceptional divisor E of blowing up O on Y points in the first infinitesimal neighborhood of O on
Y . Inductively, for i > 0, we define the points in the i-th infinitesimal neighborhood of O on Y to
be the points in the first infinitesimal neighborhood of some point in the (i − 1)-th infinitesimal
neighborhood of O. We call a point a infinitely near point of O on Y if it is in the i-th infinitesimal
neighborhood of O on Y for some i > 0.

Definition 2.10. Let p, q be points equal or infinitely near to O. The point q is said to be proximate

