9-                                                 Abstract. We introduce the class of plane valuations at infinity and prove an analogue
10-                                                 to the Abhyankar-Moh (semigroup) Theorem for it.
11-
12-
13-                                                                                  1. Introduction
14-                                            Between 1940 and 1960, Zariski and Abhyankar developed the theory of valuations in
15-                                         the context of the theory of singularities with the aim of proving resolution for algebraic
16-                                         schemes. Some important references are [21, 22, 23, 1, 2]. In the last years, there has
17-                                         been a resurgence of interest in valuations within this context. Valuations of fields of
18-                                         quotients of (Noetherian) regular two-dimensional local rings (R, m) centered at R, which
19-                                         are named plane valuations, are one of the best known classes of valuations. These valua-
20-                                         tions were classified by Zariski and a refinement of that classification in terms of the dual
21-                                         graphs associated with the valuations can be seen in [20] (see also [15]). This shows that
22-                                         plane valuations can be classified in a similar way to analytically irreducible plane curve
23-                                         singularities.
24-                                            A particular type among these singularities is that of the singularities at infinity of
25-                                         projective plane curves with only one place at infinity. An important result that concerns
26-                                         this type of curves is the Abhyankar-Moh (semigroup) Theorem [4, 3, 5]. It proves, under
27-                                         certain condition on the characteristic of the ground field, the existence of a finite set of
28-                                         positive integers satisfying certain properties that generates the so-called semigroup at
29:                                         infinity and that we call a δ-sequence in N>0 . A converse result is also true: given a
30:                                         δ-sequence in N>0 , ∆, there exists a curve with only one place at infinity whose semigroup
31-                                         at infinity is spanned by ∆. One can see a proof in [19] for the complex field and, for any
32-                                         field, it can be deduced from [6] (see [14] and references therein).
33-                                            The proximity between plane valuations and curve singularities and the fact that the
34-                                         singularity at infinity of a curve with only one place at infinity can be approached by
35-                                         approximates (see Definition 2.3) suggest the possibility of defining plane valuations at
36:                                         infinity (Definition 3.4), suitable (generalized) δ-sequences and to prove for them an ana-
37-                                         logue to the Abhyankar-Moh Theorem and its converse. We only assume that the field
38-                                         k := R/m is perfect. If one restricts to valuations where k is the field C of complex
39-                                         numbers and the value group is included in the set of real numbers, then plane valuations
40-                                         at infinity form part of the so-called valuations centered at infinity introduced in [10] to
41-                                         study the dynamics of polynomial mappings from C2 to C2 (see also [11]).
42-
43-                                           Key words and phrases. Plane valuations at infinity, curves with only one place at infinity, Abhyankar-
44-                                         Moh Theorem.
