49-   Coding Theory is an applied matter for which the theory of plane curves with only
50-one place at infinity is useful (see [7] for instance). Also, good codes can be obtained if
51:concepts as plane valuations at infinity and attached δ-sequences are used. This was done
52-by the authors in [14]; however the nature of that paper (focused on Coding Theory) did
53-not allow us to completely show the extension to plane valuations of the above mentioned
54-results concerning plane curves with only one place at infinity. In fact, we only proved
55-the converse of the Abhyankar-Moh Theorem for those types of valuations that were
56-useful for our purposes. This paper is devoted to develop the mentioned suggestion in
57-a self-contained manner, proving an analogue to the Abhyankar-Moh Theorem for plane
58-valuations at infinity as they were defined in [14] and completing the details, concerning
59:the definition of δ-sequence and the converse of the mentioned theorem, that were not
60-given in [14].
61-   In Section 2, we recall the concepts of plane curve with only one place at infinity
62:C, δ-sequence in N>0 and family of approximates for curves as C. We also state the
63:Abhyankar-Moh Theorem and show the existing relation between δ-sequences in N>0 and
64-maximal contact values of the singularity at infinity of curves C as above, fact that will
65-be useful in the paper. Section 3 summarizes some basic results on plane valuations,
66-introduces the class of plane valuations which we are interested in, plane valuations at
67-infinity, and describes the value semigroup of these valuations according their type. We
68:normalize these semigroups to adapt them to the concept of δ-sequence for our class
69-of valuations, concept that is provided in Section 4. In this section, we prove our main
70-result, Theorem 4.1, that we call Abhyankar-Moh Theorem for plane valuations at infinity,
71-whose statement goes parallel with the classical one. We also give in Remark 4.2 a nice
72-geometrical interpretation, for divisorial valuations at infinity, of the well-ordering of the
