197-3.1. Generalities on plane valuations. To begin with, we recall the general concept
198-of valuation.
199-Definition 3.1. A valuation of a field K is a mapping
200-                                     ν : K ∗ (:= K \ {0}) → G,
201-where G is a totally ordered group, such that it satisfies
202-      • ν(f + g) ≥ min{ν(f ), ν(g)} and
203-      • ν(f g) = ν(f ) + ν(g),
204-f, g being elements in K ∗ . The subring of K, Rν := {f ∈ K ∗ |ν(f ) ≥ 0} ∪ {0}, is called
205-the valuation ring of ν. Rν is a local ring whose maximal ideal is mν := {f ∈ K ∗ |ν(f ) >
206-0} ∪ {0}.
207-   Given a local regular domain (R, m), we will say that a valuation ν of the quotient
208-field of R is centered at R if R ⊆ Rν and R ∩ mν = m. The subset of G given by
209-Sν := ν(R \ {0}) is called semigroup of the valuation ν (relative to R). In the rest of the
210-paper we will only consider plane valuations, that is, valuations of the quotient field of
211-a local regular domain (R, m) of dimension two which are centered at R. Assume for a
212-while that the field k := R/m is algebraically closed. In this case, plane valuations are
213-the algebraic version of a sequence of point blowing-ups (see [20, 15] for details). In fact,
214-attached to a plane valuation ν, there is a unique sequence of point blowing-ups
215-                                   πN+1                 1        π
216-(3)                · · · −→ XN +1 −→ XN −→ · · · −→ X1 −→ X0 = Spec R,
217-where π1 is the blowing-up of X0 centered at its closed point p0 and, for each i ≥ 1, πi+1 is
218-the blowing-up of Xi at the unique closed point pi of the exceptional divisor Ei (obtained
219-after the blowing-up πi ) satisfying that ν is centered at the local ring OXi ,pi (:= Ri ).
220-Conversely, each sequence as in (3) provides a unique plane valuation. We will denote by
221-Cν = {pi }i≥0 the sequence (finite or infinite) of closed points involved in the blowing-ups
222-of (3). When Cν is finite, ν is called the divisorial valuation corresponding to the last
223-exceptional divisor obtained in (3); this is so since if πN +1 is the last blowing-up in the
224-6                              C. GALINDO AND F. MONSERRAT
225-
226-sequence (3) given by ν, then ν is the mN -adic valuation, mN being the maximal ideal of
227-the ring RN . Otherwise (when Cν is not finite), the plane valuation ν can be regarded as
228-the limit of the sequence of divisorial valuations {νi }i≥0 , νi being the divisorial valuation
229-corresponding to the divisor Ei .
230-   With the above notation, let pi and pj be points in Cν = {pi }i≥0 . We will say that
231-pi is proximate to pj (and it will be denoted by pi → pj ) if i > j and pi belongs to the
232-strict transform (by the corresponding sequence of blowing-ups given in (3)) of Ej+1 . This
233-binary relation among the points of Cν will be called proximity relation and it induces a
234-binary relation Pν in the set of natural numbers (i → j if pi → pj ). Also, the point pi is
235-said to be satellite if there exists j < i − 1 such that pi → pj (in other words, if pi belongs
236-to the intersection of the strict transforms of two exceptional divisors); otherwise, pi is
237-said to be a free point. It is worth pointing out that the semigroup Sν of a plane valuation
238-depends only on the relation Pν . According with this relation, a plane valuation ν (with
239-associated sequence Cν = {pi }i≥0 ) belongs to one of the following five types (see [20] and
240-[13]):
241-   – TYPE A: if Cν is finite.
242-   – TYPE B: if there exists i0 ∈ N>0 such that the point pi is free for all i > i0 .
243-   – TYPE C: if there exists i0 ∈ N>0 such that pi → pi0 for all i > i0 .
244-   – TYPE D: if there exists i0 ∈ N>0 such that pi is a satellite point for all i > i0 but
245-ν is not a type C valuation. This means that the sequence (3) ends with infinitely many
246-blowing-ups at satellite points, but they are not ever centered at some point of the strict
247-transforms of the same divisor.
248-   – TYPE E: if the sequence Cν alternates indefinitely blocks of free and satellite points.
249-
250-  In [9], valuations of types B, C, D and E are named, respectively, curve, exceptional
251-curve, irrational and infinitely singular valuations.
