282-We review here some basic notions from the local theory of algebraic curves.
283-For more details we refer the reader to [Sei2].
284-Let f ∈ K[[x, y]] be a non-zero power series without constant term. An
285-algebroid curve {f = 0} is deﬁned to be the ideal generated by f in K[[x, y]].
286-We say that {f = 0} is irreducible (reduced) if f in K[[x, y]] is irreducible
287-(f has no multiple factors). The irreducible curves are also called branches.
288-The order ord f of the power series f is, by deﬁnition, the multiplicity of
289-the curve {f = 0}. The initial form inf of f deﬁnes the tangent lines of
290-{f = 0}. If {f = 0} is irreducible then it has only one tangent line i.e.
291-inf = lord f where l is a linear form.
