+### 3.3 Piece 2 has an exact counterexample
+
+Set
+
+```text
+p(t)=t^6+(3/2)t^3+3/8,       q(t)=t^4+t.
+```
+
+Then
+
+```text
+p^2-q^3=(1/8)t^3+9/64.
+```
+
+Hence `z=t^3=8(p^2-q^3)-9/8` lies in `C[p,q]`, and
+
+```text
+C[p,q]=C[z,y]/(y^3-z(z+1)^3),   y=q=t(z+1).
+```
+
+Its fraction field contains `t=y/(z+1)`, so the map is birational, its normalization is `A^1`, and only `t=infinity` lies over infinity.  Thus it is a polynomial curve of type `(d,n)=(6,4)`.  The monic normal form in `y` has basis `1,y,y^2` over `C[z]`; the leading degrees `3i+4j` for `j=0,1,2` are in distinct residue classes modulo 3.  Therefore `S_aff=<3,4>` exactly and `delta_aff=3`.
+
+At infinity put `u=1/p`, `v=q/p`, and `s=1/t`.  Then `v=s^2*unit`, and the exact identity
+
+```text
+u-v^3=(p^2-q^3)/p^3=s^15*unit
+```
+
